00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:15,830
I wanted to take a step back and clarify the context of the risk.

18
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:20,910
For a layperson, the use of LLM is usually asking ChatGPT or Gemini some question,

19
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:24,870
asking it to analyze some data, or asking it to create an image for you.

20
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,030
Since this is interfacing directly with the LLM,

21
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:31,150
am I right in assuming there is no security risk here.

22
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:33,550
and the focus is rather on organizations.

23
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:38,830
that have built applications on top of a large language model or a small language model?

24
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:41,670
Yeah, I mean, it's a great question.

25
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:46,710
Let me try to give a little broader context and answer the question.

26
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:50,630
LLMs are basically models which are trained on data up to a certain amount of time.

27
00:01:50,630 --> 00:01:56,310
So, they typically cannot answer questions on current events like stock price.

28
00:01:56,310 --> 00:01:58,510
or news events and so on and so forth.

29
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:05,990
And in case of a consumer application, it is usually about asking LLMs about some information,

30
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:09,029
and it is about things which are baked into a foundation model.

31
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:16,230
And when we talk about foundation model, these are models which are trained on internet scale data, on all kinds of data and information.

32
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,030
Consumer use case is predominantly about augmenting these LLMs.

33
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:23,430
which have been trained up to a certain amount of time with current information.

34
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:27,990
because they are, as I mentioned, not aware of current information.

35
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:35,471
Whereas, in case of enterprises, the use case is mostly about augmenting these LLMs with input from enterprise. specific data.

36
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:42,230
data that is sitting in enterprise data lakes, document stores, enterprise applications.

37
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:46,150
which are usually restricted by access control measures, and so on and so forth.

38
00:02:46,150 --> 00:02:47,830
With regard to consumers, the extra data.

39
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:53,110
that is being augmented is still mostly public data or users' personal data.

40
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:56,949
But in case of enterprise data, the risks of the information.

41
00:02:56,949 --> 00:02:59,710
that is being sent to the LLM has different implications.

42
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:04,670
It could be data from role or group of internal users,

43
00:03:04,670 --> 00:03:09,750
it could be sensitive customer data, company proprietary information, IP, and so on and so forth.

44
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:14,229
And hence, the risk level of interfacing with LLMs in case of consumer applications,

45
00:03:14,239 --> 00:03:21,710
enterprise applications, really is all about what kind of data is being exposed to the LLM.

46
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:25,630
and what kind of data is being leveraged by the LLM in order to answer the question.

47
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,111
Hope that makes sense. It does.

48
00:03:28,111 --> 00:03:29,990
So, what you're saying is that there is risk,

49
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:34,790
but the level of risk is different based on higher on the enterprise end.

50
00:03:34,790 --> 00:03:39,071
and a bit lower on the consumer facing generic LLMs. Absolutely.

51
00:03:39,071 --> 00:03:41,710
I mean, there is still lies.

52
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:45,229
I mean, users are still at risk of exposing their own personal information to the applications of the.

53
00:03:45,229 --> 00:03:48,070
likes ChatGPT.

54
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:53,271
I mean, hopefully nobody is asking what their credit score is by providing their social security number to. ChatGPT.

55
00:03:53,271 --> 00:03:57,670
So, there is risk, but the risk is really about users own personal information.

56
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:01,670
that they're accidentally disclosing to the generative AI applications.

57
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:07,830
Whereas, in case of enterprise application, the risk is magnified because it's not just about users personal information.

58
00:04:07,830 --> 00:04:13,630
but it is also about other users information, aka customer information,

59
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:24,310
or proprietary information about financials of the company, or sensitive intellectual property information, code, secrets and tokens, etc.

60
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:28,790
Which has, as I mentioned, really different lens to the risk and magnitude to the risk.

61
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,990
Thanks, that explains it.

62
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,070
Going on on the same theme, what is it about LLMs.

63
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:40,310
that make them so powerful and also risky compared to traditional software components?

64
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:51,550
LLMs are traditionally generative AI models, which have an awesome ability to interpret unstructured text.

65
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:56,230
and ability to predict next tokens on the basis of the history of tokens it has seen.

66
00:04:56,230 --> 00:05:06,390
and the ability to produce content which looks and mimics human text is really what makes LLM really,

67
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:09,030
really compelling for consumers.

68
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:15,270
So, it emulates a conversational experience for users because users can continue to interact and ask questions.

69
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:17,110
and on the basis of the history that it's able to analyze,

70
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:18,750
it's able to carry on a conversation.

71
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:25,310
Because it can answer the second question on the basis of continuity of the information.

72
00:05:25,310 --> 00:05:26,990
that it was collecting based on previous questions.

73
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:31,510
and answers that were given in a conversational style interaction with the LLMs.

74
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:37,470
So, the whole conversational experience that is now possible by LLMs with huge memory.

75
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:42,990
and context windows really makes LLM very unique.

76
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:46,750
and powerful and just very easy to use by any and all kinds of users.

77
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:52,190
It does not require technical expertise, it does not require programming experience, and so on and so forth.

78
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:57,110
It serves the needs of all technical versus non-technical audience in a very easy-to-use fashion.

79
00:05:57,110 --> 00:06:02,310
That property of the LLMs is really what enables.

80
00:06:02,310 --> 00:06:04,230
and empowers its success both in consumer as well as enterprise world.

81
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:08,350
So, typically when writing a software application traditionally,

82
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:13,070
we would be restricting the ways in which the input can be handled by the application.

83
00:06:13,070 --> 00:06:17,350
It's has been traditionally very hard to handle unstructured text.

84
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:20,909
You have kind of have to build in a lot of text processing logic.

85
00:06:20,909 --> 00:06:25,791
You know, the output is usually very structured, and you know. you used to code a lot of ways in.

86
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:31,150
which the output could be made more easy to understand for the users.

87
00:06:31,150 --> 00:06:35,950
Whereas by using LLMs, we are able to process unstructured.

88
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:43,430
or structured data and present back to the user information in a very easy-to-grasp and comprehend format.

89
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:47,390
And its ability to represent information with different levels of complexity,

90
00:06:47,390 --> 00:06:51,110
in different styles, leveraging the vast amount of information that it has learned.

91
00:06:51,110 --> 00:06:56,909
it really empowers LLM-based applications to be so successful in both consumer and enterprise scenarios.

92
00:06:56,919 --> 00:07:03,630
We'll go to the next team, which is around understanding the key risks for LLM applications,

93
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:05,670
and we'll use the OWASP.

94
00:07:05,670 --> 00:07:10,870
which is the Open Web Application Security Project Top 10 risks that have been articulated for LLMs.

95
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:16,110
OWASP covers the most important threat vectors for generative AI applications,

96
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:20,230
and they have some really awesome material on their website with sites.

97
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:25,230
you know, more details on these these attacks, examples of these attacks, mitigation techniques.

98
00:07:25,230 --> 00:07:27,470
and so on and so forth.

99
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:30,070
I'll briefly cover the top 10 here,

100
00:07:30,070 --> 00:07:34,750
and we can dig into any any of these subjects in more details as you wish.

101
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:36,830
The first one is basically prompt injection attacks.

102
00:07:36,830 --> 00:07:40,510
Prompt injection and jailbreaks are often kind of synonymously used terms,

103
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:46,590
but basically prompt injection is about how the user input can manipulate.

104
00:07:46,590 --> 00:07:51,870
and change the behavior of the AI application to respond to the user's question.

105
00:07:51,870 --> 00:07:57,310
and thereby carrying out actions which are really unintended by the AI application.

106
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:01,350
Whereas jailbreak is all about bypassing the guardrails.

107
00:08:01,350 --> 00:08:07,430
which might have been baked into the LLM to prevent it from and disclosing certain kinds of information.

108
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:11,670
So prompt injection attack is really the top risk.

109
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:15,150
because of its ability to leak sensitive information.

110
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:19,470
or carry out actions that were really not intended by the AI application.

111
00:08:19,470 --> 00:08:24,630
The second risk really is around sensitive information disclosure.

112
00:08:24,630 --> 00:08:34,550
The LLMs in case of enterprise or enterprise scenarios as I discussed is all about augmenting the LLM. with enterprise specific data. And that can be achieved by multiple ways.

113
00:08:34,550 --> 00:08:39,469
I mean you can train your models with the enterprise data.

114
00:08:39,469 --> 00:08:43,990
or you can take an existing model and fine tune it with providing enterprise specific data.

115
00:08:43,990 --> 00:08:49,870
or you can provide the enterprise specific data in the context of the application input.

116
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:52,430
and then expect the answers that you wanted the LLM to give.

117
00:08:52,430 --> 00:08:53,950
Now in the first two scenarios where you're.

118
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:58,190
either training the model or fine tuning the model it is really about the information.

119
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:00,110
that is going into the model.

120
00:09:00,110 --> 00:09:03,829
Now this information that is being that you're using to train.

121
00:09:03,829 --> 00:09:10,110
or fine tune the model could be extracted from enterprise applications which had access controls and authorizations.

122
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:13,990
and different levels of users, roles of users had different kinds of access.

123
00:09:13,990 --> 00:09:15,990
but you're putting all of that together into a single model.

124
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:23,950
and thereby risking accidentally leaking information that was otherwise unauthorized for certain users in the source application.

125
00:09:23,950 --> 00:09:26,750
So that is an example of the risk.

126
00:09:26,750 --> 00:09:30,870
that comes via the sensitive information disclosure risk that has been identified by OWASP.

127
00:09:30,870 --> 00:09:33,710
The third one is around supply chain.

128
00:09:33,710 --> 00:09:38,390
Again, uh fine tuning or augmenting the user input with context.

129
00:09:38,390 --> 00:09:41,910
or training the model is all about where you're sourcing the data from.

130
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:44,670
And if you're sourcing the data from untrusted, unverified sources,

131
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:50,030
then you are susceptible to things like biases,

132
00:09:50,030 --> 00:09:54,870
or misinformation, or conflicting information, and so on and so forth.

133
00:09:54,870 --> 00:10:00,070
and thereby uh leading to really degraded results coming out of the LLM.

134
00:10:00,070 --> 00:10:03,670
because it's just confused at at that point, or is giving you incorrect information.

135
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:08,070
Now, the fourth risk is around data and model poisoning,

136
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:12,190
which really specifically talks about data that is being used for training and fine-tuning.

137
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:15,950
and leading to things like biases, and so on and so forth,

138
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:21,430
which are otherwise very hard to detect, and can lead to unexpected or incorrect outcomes.

139
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:26,150
Excessive agency, which is the sixth the sixth risk,

140
00:10:26,150 --> 00:10:31,510
is mostly about things like generative AI applications, agentic specifically.

141
00:10:31,510 --> 00:10:36,510
where these applications are really designed to serve a diverse set of user needs.

142
00:10:36,510 --> 00:10:39,270
And user types in an enterprise scenario,

143
00:10:39,270 --> 00:10:45,230
and typically in an enterprise scenario, different users interfacing with these AI applications have different levels of access.

144
00:10:45,230 --> 00:10:47,230
control and authorization.

145
00:10:47,230 --> 00:10:53,590
and hence, by definition of being able to serve to a wide variety of users, these applications.

146
00:10:53,590 --> 00:10:56,990
and agents are usually provisioned with high levels of privilege.

147
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:59,350
and access tokens so that they can serve the needs of diverse users.

148
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:08,230
And that in itself poses a risk where the agent can potentially perform privileged access to actions.

149
00:11:08,230 --> 00:11:10,230
or can access privileged information that was.

150
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:14,829
otherwise not permissible to the user in the source application in the first place.

151
00:11:14,839 --> 00:11:17,590
So, that's the sixth risk.

152
00:11:17,590 --> 00:11:19,910
The seventh one is around system prompt leakage.

153
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:28,350
Again, LLMs really the generative AI applications have uh two forms of input to the LLM.

154
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:30,670
The system prompt is usually an instruction.

155
00:11:30,670 --> 00:11:32,870
that directs the LLM to behave and respond in a certain way.

156
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:37,790
Whereas a user prompt is about the input that the user provides to the application.

157
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:43,350
And these two things are combined and sent to the LLM to respond in in a particular way.

158
00:11:43,350 --> 00:11:47,230
that was expected by the developer or the admin of the application.

159
00:11:47,230 --> 00:11:51,070
Now, system prompt is purely instructional, should not contain any sensitive information.

160
00:11:51,070 --> 00:11:56,510
And nonetheless, we have seen ample examples where system prompt has sensitive information.

161
00:11:56,510 --> 00:12:02,270
In case of consumer applications, these could be things like discount codes or or marketing campaign information, etc.

162
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:05,590
And in case of enterprise applications, these could be sensitive information.

163
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:09,630
that could have been embedded in the system prompt via.

164
00:12:09,630 --> 00:12:13,150
you know, examples that you're presenting to the LLM to respond into in a certain way.

165
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:15,950
and so on and so forth.

166
00:12:15,950 --> 00:12:22,110
So, if the user is able to prompt the LLM to return back what the system prompt was.

167
00:12:22,110 --> 00:12:26,350
they can learn a lot more about the application boundaries, maybe sensitive information.

168
00:12:26,350 --> 00:12:28,470
and so on and so forth, and.

169
00:12:28,470 --> 00:12:34,031
then craft an attack that clearly tries to evade what the system boundaries were being enforced by the. system prompt.

170
00:12:34,031 --> 00:12:38,390
So, system prompt leakage is a risk only when you have all of these kind of elements,

171
00:12:38,390 --> 00:12:42,070
sensitive pieces of information in the system prompt itself.

172
00:12:42,080 --> 00:12:46,670
Now, the eighth is around vector and embedding weaknesses.

173
00:12:46,670 --> 00:12:53,030
This is all about how vectors and embeddings are generated, you know, or stored or retrieved.

174
00:12:53,030 --> 00:12:55,750
This attack vector is mostly applicable to RAG applications,

175
00:12:55,750 --> 00:13:03,750
which typically are about retrieving relevant piece of information from a stored data repository like a vector DB.

176
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:08,430
And in the process of retrieval, it is able to retrieve information.

177
00:13:08,430 --> 00:13:15,430
that was above and beyond user's authorized level in the source application from which the data was retrieved.

178
00:13:15,430 --> 00:13:17,829
So, vector and embedding weakness is about simply understanding.

179
00:13:17,839 --> 00:13:27,070
and exploiting the weaknesses of the embedding techniques that allows the attacker to retrieve more than authorized information.

180
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:30,470
Now, the ninth risk is about misinformation.

181
00:13:30,470 --> 00:13:32,110
You know, false or misleading information that is generated.

182
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:40,590
because the LLMs are really trying to fill in the gaps of information that either it.

183
00:13:40,590 --> 00:13:42,270
does not have on the base of the data it has been trained.

184
00:13:42,270 --> 00:13:44,870
or the context that it has been provided.

185
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:48,990
and it tries to fill in the gap using statistical methods.

186
00:13:48,990 --> 00:13:54,150
And then again, misinformation is really an attack on the user of the application.

187
00:13:54,150 --> 00:14:01,271
because the excellent way in which LLMs generate the data can be very convincing to the user about. what the information is.

188
00:14:01,271 --> 00:14:05,670
and thereby trick the user to act on that information.

189
00:14:05,670 --> 00:14:07,870
The last risk is really about unbounded consumption,

190
00:14:07,870 --> 00:14:15,710
which is really caused by uncontrolled or excessive inferences that could be triggered on the LLM.

191
00:14:15,710 --> 00:14:21,870
This could be as simple as user asking the AI application to solve a puzzle.

192
00:14:21,870 --> 00:14:26,430
And the LLM, although was designed to be helpful system support agent,

193
00:14:26,430 --> 00:14:30,630
would be busy solving a puzzle thereby causing both an unbounded consumption.

194
00:14:30,630 --> 00:14:34,911
but at the same time a denial of service on other legitimate users. and use cases of the application.

195
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:41,150
So, these are the top 10 risks that have been identified by OWASP for generative AI application.

196
00:14:41,150 --> 00:14:43,430
Hopefully, that gives you some good understanding of the risks.

197
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:47,710
and the breadth of the risks that are presented by generative AI applications.

198
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:50,550
It does.

199
00:14:50,550 --> 00:14:52,829
Does any example or specific example come to mind.

200
00:14:52,829 --> 00:14:56,110
where any of these risks have manifested in a real deployment?

201
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:01,390
Yeah. We can take very specific examples around prompt injection and jailbreak.

202
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:09,230
So, let's double-click on prompt injection and then in that context, I will clearly explain a real-world attack.

203
00:15:09,230 --> 00:15:16,190
So, as I mentioned, prompt injection attacks are all about disguising malicious instructions as benign inputs trying to.

204
00:15:16,190 --> 00:15:19,870
alter the behavior or output in unintended ways.

205
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:24,510
Whereas jailbreaking is all about making an LLM ignore its own safeguards.

206
00:15:24,510 --> 00:15:29,110
In some cases, LLMs have safeguards baked in for things like,

207
00:15:29,110 --> 00:15:35,230
you know, not engaging in self-harm and violence kind of conversations.

208
00:15:35,230 --> 00:15:39,350
Whereas an LLM would try to avoid answering questions related to that.

209
00:15:39,350 --> 00:15:45,550
Prompt injection exploits the fact that LLMs really don't distinguish between the developer-provided instructions.

210
00:15:45,550 --> 00:15:50,030
or admin instructions or system prompt that I was explaining and the user input.

211
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:54,190
And both of them are just treated as input tokens on which the LLM tries to act.

212
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:56,430
So, if the user can provide an input.

213
00:15:56,440 --> 00:16:01,830
that can make the LLM understand the data as if it was developer instructions.

214
00:16:01,840 --> 00:16:07,150
or system prompt instructions, it can cause the LLM to do things that were otherwise restricted.

215
00:16:07,150 --> 00:16:10,790
Now, there are various kinds of prompt injection attacks.

216
00:16:10,790 --> 00:16:14,110
The two most commonly talked about prompt injection attacks are direct and indirect.

217
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:18,870
A direct prompt injection attack is referred to the injection tokens.

218
00:16:18,870 --> 00:16:21,550
which are part of the user input directly.

219
00:16:21,550 --> 00:16:23,430
So, when the user is asking question,

220
00:16:23,430 --> 00:16:28,470
they can instruct the LLM with things like ignore previous instructions,

221
00:16:28,470 --> 00:16:30,590
this is what I want you to do.

222
00:16:30,590 --> 00:16:35,110
and it would cause the LLM to kind of ignore the system prompt instructions.

223
00:16:35,110 --> 00:16:37,670
that were set in place to start with.

224
00:16:37,670 --> 00:16:40,550
That is an example of a direct prompt injection attack.

225
00:16:40,550 --> 00:16:45,310
Whereas an indirect prompt injection attack is about when the user asks a question.

226
00:16:45,310 --> 00:16:50,790
and the LLM tries to augment the user's question with data that it retrieves from any source.

227
00:16:50,790 --> 00:16:57,670
This source of information could pull in malicious tokens, which would again then go back to the LLM.

228
00:16:57,670 --> 00:17:01,350
and the LLM would interpret them as instructions.

229
00:17:01,350 --> 00:17:03,070
that it has to follow in order to answer the question.

230
00:17:03,070 --> 00:17:09,670
So, indirect prompt injection attacks are kind of hideous in the way that users don't see it.

231
00:17:09,670 --> 00:17:13,670
They basically ask a question, and the context pulls in malicious tokens,

232
00:17:13,670 --> 00:17:16,350
and there it goes to the LLM for it to misbehave.

233
00:17:16,350 --> 00:17:21,230
Echo leak was a very recently disclosed indirect prompt injection attack.

234
00:17:21,230 --> 00:17:29,950
wherein the attacker basically sent a very benign looking email with malicious token very cleanly laid out in.

235
00:17:29,950 --> 00:17:32,590
the email such that it semantically made sense.

236
00:17:32,590 --> 00:17:36,510
But also constructed in such a way that in response to the user's question,

237
00:17:36,510 --> 00:17:41,550
all of these malicious tokens were pulled into the context.

238
00:17:41,550 --> 00:17:44,790
and sent to Copilot for it to.

239
00:17:44,790 --> 00:17:49,990
then do malicious instructions or actions as directed by the malicious tokens embedded in the email.

240
00:17:49,990 --> 00:17:55,070
So, again, the attacker sends a very benign looking email with malicious token.

241
00:17:55,070 --> 00:17:57,870
The user asks to summarize the email.

242
00:17:57,870 --> 00:18:03,710
