00:24:23,710 --> 00:24:31,551
and output content inspection guardrails that would automatically redact sensitive information from the LLM responses like the secret. phrase.

333
00:24:31,560 --> 00:24:33,990
So, some added level of difficulty there.

334
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:41,870
and then the third room was system prompt guardrails with input and output inspection guardrails.

335
00:24:41,870 --> 00:24:44,550
and then added on to that was prompt injection detections.

336
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:51,310
where the guardrails were using very specifically designed prompt injection detection techniques such.

337
00:24:51,310 --> 00:24:53,590
that the attack can be stopped either.

338
00:24:53,590 --> 00:24:55,270
on the way into the application on the.

339
00:24:55,270 --> 00:24:58,030
or on the way out of the application.

340
00:24:58,030 --> 00:25:03,830
And so, it had different levels of difficulties by that by nature and by design.

341
00:25:03,830 --> 00:25:09,710
On room one, we approximately saw one in five players successfully overcoming system prompt guardrails.

342
00:25:09,720 --> 00:25:15,270
That is how easy and trivial it is to bypass the most common set of guardrails.

343
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:19,350
that are put in place by most application developers.

344
00:25:19,350 --> 00:25:26,751
In case of room two, approximately four in 100 players came the first four levels of content guardrails. that were put in place.

345
00:25:26,751 --> 00:25:35,030
And in room three, only one player was able to successfully evade all of the defenses.

346
00:25:35,030 --> 00:25:40,190
and only three in 100,000 attempts on an average was able to beat this final guardrail.

347
00:25:40,190 --> 00:25:42,190
So, very different levels of complexity.

348
00:25:42,190 --> 00:25:49,550
Room one represents really the majority of AI applications that are designed and put in place.

349
00:25:49,550 --> 00:25:51,190
And room two and room three are additional guardrails.

350
00:25:51,190 --> 00:25:55,670
that are put in place with more security considerations in mind.

351
00:25:55,670 --> 00:25:59,230
And so, there are a lot of different interesting characteristics of the successful attack.

352
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:04,190
that led to the success of the attack, and I'll briefly touch on three things.

353
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:13,070
First of all, he incorporated a technique called distractor instructions, where he bookended his bookended his prompt.

354
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:17,310
which can help mask the true intent of the prompt,

355
00:26:17,310 --> 00:26:21,430
thereby lowering the internal scoring of the suspicious content.

356
00:26:21,430 --> 00:26:25,230
and making it hard for filters or LLM classifiers to detect the injection.

357
00:26:25,230 --> 00:26:27,711
So, that was his first technique. How do you do that?

358
00:26:27,711 --> 00:26:32,070
If you can just expand on that, how do you bookend your prompt?

359
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:41,870
You would provide instructions to the LLM repetitively, and you would put in instructions before your malicious instructions.

360
00:26:41,870 --> 00:26:47,310
and after your malicious instructions to confuse the LLM, the detection techniques,

361
00:26:47,310 --> 00:26:52,790
or LLM filters in order to detect what is really going on in the prompt.

362
00:26:52,790 --> 00:26:57,470
So, you would repeat, and you would put in confusing instructions at the beginning.

363
00:26:57,470 --> 00:27:03,630
and at the end of the prompt, which is really trying to perform the prompt injection.

364
00:27:03,640 --> 00:27:08,070
The second technique was around cognitive hacking.

365
00:27:08,070 --> 00:27:13,150
where the components he included appeal to the LLM's tendency to evaluate previous and statements,

366
00:27:13,160 --> 00:27:16,070
encouraging it it to lower its guard.

367
00:27:16,070 --> 00:27:20,830
and comply while also subtly nudging the model to validate.

368
00:27:20,830 --> 00:27:25,390
and reinforce the attacker's instructions by embedding them in reasoning steps.

369
00:27:25,390 --> 00:27:32,590
So, this is about playing around with LLM's reasoning techniques in order to lower its guardrail over a.

370
00:27:32,590 --> 00:27:37,311
sequence of instructions that are given to the LLM in the attack. itself.

371
00:27:37,311 --> 00:27:40,070
And finally, there is he uses style injection.

372
00:27:40,070 --> 00:27:46,471
where the core payload in his prompt is really designed to modify the output format such. that the model can leak.

373
00:27:46,471 --> 00:27:49,070
the private data and evade the content filters.

374
00:27:49,070 --> 00:27:54,150
And so, that really is a very common technique.

375
00:27:54,150 --> 00:28:00,430
where you can request the LLM to form the output in creative ways that can evade content filters.

376
00:28:00,430 --> 00:28:05,550
So, you could ask it to encode the data with a specific encoding scheme.

377
00:28:05,550 --> 00:28:09,550
that would evade content filters that have been put in place.

378
00:28:09,550 --> 00:28:13,750
So, if you're looking for a sequence of numbers, the evasion technique could be about interlacing.

379
00:28:13,750 --> 00:28:21,670
or interpolating the output with characters or speaking out the numbers as words and so on so forth.

380
00:28:21,670 --> 00:28:25,550
So, these are very cute and common techniques that are used in order to evade a filter,

381
00:28:25,550 --> 00:28:28,390
for example, which is only looking for a sequence of numbers.

382
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:33,110
And we learned a lot from this game that we had hosted.

383
00:28:33,110 --> 00:28:37,590
All kinds of tokenization exploit techniques that were used, we learned that it kind of new,

384
00:28:37,590 --> 00:28:41,550
but it really brought to the forefront things like, you know.

385
00:28:41,550 --> 00:28:43,150
when LLM is trying to interpret the words,

