668
00:49:10,040 --> 00:49:15,190
So, how should developers account for this in their security architecture?

669
00:49:15,200 --> 00:49:20,390
So, that's a really good question, and it touches on many different topics.

670
00:49:20,390 --> 00:49:26,390
So, LLMs and generative AI models, as the name implies,

671
00:49:26,390 --> 00:49:29,910
are generative in nature in the sense that they try to generate.

672
00:49:29,920 --> 00:49:38,390
aka predict, the next set of tokens in order to suffice the input question that has been asked.

673
00:49:38,390 --> 00:49:40,750
And in order to generate the next set of tokens,

674
00:49:40,750 --> 00:49:46,590
it basically is using all the information that it has learned and has been provided as an input.

675
00:49:46,600 --> 00:49:52,430
Now, when analyzing the input and the information that it has been trained on,

676
00:49:52,440 --> 00:49:59,190
it is limited to what it has been trained on and what input is being provided.

677
00:49:59,200 --> 00:50:01,590
And when analyzing the history of input,

678
00:50:01,600 --> 00:50:05,910
it is limited by the amount of memory it has to collect this input.

679
00:50:05,910 --> 00:50:08,590
so that it can answer to the user's question.

680
00:50:08,590 --> 00:50:11,870
So now, the unpredictability of the LLMs,

681
00:50:11,870 --> 00:50:19,230
also referred to as hallucination, misinformation, you know, many different ways of calling out the same problem.

682
00:50:19,230 --> 00:50:27,751
is based on the fact that when the information provided to the LLM has gaps, the LLM tries to predict.

683
00:50:27,751 --> 00:50:30,070
and generate the best possible answer.

684
00:50:30,070 --> 00:50:35,550
And that could in some cases be completely incorrect,

685
00:50:35,550 --> 00:50:41,910
but because the LLMs generate the output in semantically correct fashion, it would look very convincingly correct to the user.

686
00:50:41,920 --> 00:50:46,990
So, when we talk about unpredictability of the LLMs,

687
00:50:46,990 --> 00:50:52,630
it can in fact be controlled to a certain extent by certain parameters of the API calls.

688
00:50:52,630 --> 00:50:56,590
You know, when you're asking a question to the LLM,

689
00:50:56,590 --> 00:50:59,550
you can ask it to be not so generative, not be so creative.

690
00:50:59,550 --> 00:51:02,990
and stick to the facts, and so on and so forth.

691
00:51:02,990 --> 00:51:05,670
So, there are input parameters like temperature and top P and so on.

692
00:51:05,670 --> 00:51:14,910
and so forth that reduces its ability to use statistical techniques to potentially predict tokens.

693
00:51:14,910 --> 00:51:17,110
when it is not found in the data that it was using.

694
00:51:17,110 --> 00:51:19,990
So, that is one way.

695
00:51:19,990 --> 00:51:26,910
The other is an attack which actually exploits the memory capacity of the AI application.

696
00:51:26,910 --> 00:51:30,190
where the input is so huge or builds up over period of time.

697
00:51:30,200 --> 00:51:32,390
that the initial set of guardrails.

698
00:51:32,390 --> 00:51:34,550
or instructions that were given to the LLM,

699
00:51:34,560 --> 00:51:39,070
that the LLM was taking into account to process and generate the output,

700
00:51:39,070 --> 00:51:41,790
simply slip out of its memory, right?

701
00:51:41,790 --> 00:51:43,990
So, let's assume that your memory is 100 words.

702
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:48,670
and you have given the instructions to not do X.

703
00:51:48,670 --> 00:51:53,710
and then you augment the user question, but the user question itself is 100, you know, words.

704
00:51:53,710 --> 00:52:00,671
It would mean that the instructions that were trying to enforce certain constraints simply move out of the. memory window.

705
00:52:00,671 --> 00:52:05,710
And so now when the LLM is trying to answer the question, it does not even take into account those constraints.

706
00:52:05,710 --> 00:52:09,230
because they have been simply pushed out of the window.

707
00:52:09,230 --> 00:52:11,390
It is then only paying attention to the last 100 words,

708
00:52:11,390 --> 00:52:15,190
and there in there are no constraints, and then it starts answering the question.

709
00:52:15,190 --> 00:52:18,350
So, there are different ways in which the LLMs are.

710
00:52:18,360 --> 00:52:24,070
then perceived to misbehave or go off guards.

711
00:52:24,070 --> 00:52:26,590
or off rails when trying to answer to a user's question.

712
00:52:26,590 --> 00:52:30,870
And the protection techniques are really all about making sure.

713
00:52:30,870 --> 00:52:33,910
that you use the right parameters for the right application.

714
00:52:33,920 --> 00:52:35,670
You know, you can also, you know,

715
00:52:35,670 --> 00:52:39,350
use techniques like citing the exact source of information back to the user.

716
00:52:39,350 --> 00:52:44,630
when answering the question in your AI application. so that the user is assured of the fact.

717
00:52:44,630 --> 00:52:49,390
that these are coming from some factual source of information rather than being generated on the fly.

718
00:52:49,400 --> 00:52:55,350
And then in order to protect against the memory size attacks.

719
00:52:55,350 --> 00:53:02,270
it's about how do you continue to capture the history of the conversation within the limits of the.

720
00:53:02,270 --> 00:53:07,310
memory by using techniques like summarization or most relevant tokens, etc.

721
00:53:07,310 --> 00:53:11,190
So that you make sure that the most relevant pieces of instructions are never thrown out of the.

722
00:53:11,190 --> 00:53:12,870
window or go over the limit of the memory.

723
00:53:12,870 --> 00:53:14,630
And there are other more creative techniques.

724
00:53:14,640 --> 00:53:19,910
There's a papers around how you can repeat the system instructions at the end of the user prompt.

725
00:53:19,910 --> 00:53:23,790
to make sure that the system instructions are always within the memory.

726
00:53:23,790 --> 00:53:25,310
window of the AI application.

727
00:53:25,310 --> 00:53:30,470
So, there are lots of different interesting techniques that can be used. Hopefully that gives some interesting color.

728
00:53:30,480 --> 00:53:36,790
It does. Beyond technical guardrails, are there any other actions.

729
00:53:36,790 --> 00:53:41,790
that security team can take or the development team can take to improve the security posture?

730
00:53:41,790 --> 00:53:46,830
Yeah, we talked about differences between consumer applications and enterprise applications.

731
00:53:46,830 --> 00:53:49,110
And as I mentioned at the beginning,

732
00:53:49,110 --> 00:53:58,510
the risks about enterprise applications are purely the data that is being sourced from various internal applications.

733
00:53:58,510 --> 00:54:01,710
and being augmented to the user input.

734
00:54:01,710 --> 00:54:05,190
and being sent to the LLM and responding and the LLM send response back to the user.

735
00:54:05,200 --> 00:54:10,150
Therein, the first risk is about where is the data coming from?

736
00:54:10,150 --> 00:54:16,830
And if the data is coming from applications which by themselves don't have proper access controls,

737
00:54:16,830 --> 00:54:21,430
you have the risk of it potentially getting manipulated by the attacker.

738
00:54:21,430 --> 00:54:26,590
or untrusted content landing into the data source that can then be pulled by the AI application.

739
00:54:26,590 --> 00:54:32,670
and augmented to the user questions and thereby the LLM would end up responding to the users in.

740
00:54:32,670 --> 00:54:34,390
incorrect ways or with misinformation and so on and so forth.

741
00:54:34,400 --> 00:54:38,230
So, the first measure that any application developer should.

742
00:54:38,230 --> 00:54:44,830
and could exercise is about making sure that the data is sourced from vetted and verified sources.

743
00:54:44,840 --> 00:54:50,590
And if you have collected the data that you're not potentially adding any kind of risks.

744
00:54:50,590 --> 00:54:54,350
If you're building a rag application that is pulling the data from enterprise applications.

745
00:54:54,350 --> 00:54:58,310
and putting it into a vector DB, let's make sure there are no secrets.

746
00:54:58,310 --> 00:55:02,430
and tokens, there are no credit card information, there is no social security number, etc.

747
00:55:02,430 --> 00:55:09,350
landing into the vector DB because then you're increasing the potential risk of it getting extracted.

748
00:55:09,350 --> 00:55:11,750
and leaked back to the user which you never wanted, hopefully.

749
00:55:11,760 --> 00:55:16,430
So, managing the entire data pipeline, where is the data coming from,

750
00:55:16,430 --> 00:55:21,990
how it is being processed, how it is being then collected. what is being sent to the LLM,

751
00:55:22,000 --> 00:55:28,950
all the kinds of precautions that the AI application developer can use in order to make sure.

752
00:55:28,950 --> 00:55:31,390
that the risk is dramatically minimized.

753
00:55:31,400 --> 00:55:35,750
So, those are kind of the technical guardrails.

754
00:55:35,750 --> 00:55:38,750
that the user can apply in order to reduce the attack surface of the applications.

755
00:55:38,760 --> 00:55:45,750
And any proactive security testing that can be conducted to identify any vulnerabilities?

756
00:55:45,760 --> 00:55:52,990
Yeah, I mean there are quite a few open-source as well as commercial red teaming tools.

757
00:55:52,990 --> 00:55:56,750
and capabilities available.

758
00:55:56,750 --> 00:56:04,830
And I would really encourage any AI application developer just do basic common sense tests on your application.

759
00:56:04,830 --> 00:56:07,830
Make it reveal some kind of sensitive information.

760
00:56:07,830 --> 00:56:13,310
that you never wanted it to answer or reveal in an answer back to the user.

761
00:56:13,310 --> 00:56:16,470
Make it do things that you did not expect it to do.

762
00:56:16,480 --> 00:56:19,550
Basic common sense testing is would be the first step.

763
00:56:19,560 --> 00:56:27,870
Then using open-source tools that you can use in order to prompt your application in order to cause.

764
00:56:27,870 --> 00:56:30,910
it to misbehave would be the second.

765
00:56:30,910 --> 00:56:37,030
And for more serious enterprise applications, there is no harm in engaging in a commercial paid,

766
00:56:37,030 --> 00:56:41,990
you know, red teaming exercise on top of the application to really uncover.

767
00:56:41,990 --> 00:56:44,390
because we as developers of the application are always biased.

768
00:56:44,390 --> 00:56:49,430
and sometimes overlook some very common security measures that should have been put in place.

769
00:56:49,430 --> 00:56:51,630
We assume they're in place.

770
00:56:51,630 --> 00:56:55,790
But only through a third party do we realize that we were missing those basic guardrails.

771
00:56:55,790 --> 00:56:57,750
So, I would say take advantage of that.

772
00:56:57,750 --> 00:57:01,150
And then, you know, these open-source tools are getting pretty creative.

773
00:57:01,150 --> 00:57:04,390
They themselves leverage LLMs in order to recraft.

774
00:57:04,390 --> 00:57:09,230
and generate different variants of prompt injection tokens in order to iterate.

775
00:57:09,230 --> 00:57:12,150
and try to evade the guardrails that might have been put in place.

776
00:57:12,150 --> 00:57:18,310
So, they're getting pretty sophisticated and and very effective in identifying some basic weaknesses of the application.

777
00:57:18,310 --> 00:57:22,030
So, I'd really encourage application developers to take advantage of basic testing,

778
00:57:22,040 --> 00:57:26,590
open-source tools, commercial offerings, whatever is possible.

779
00:57:26,590 --> 00:57:30,670
But do exercise these basic vulnerability tests on your applications in order to make.

780
00:57:30,670 --> 00:57:34,430
sure they are really a for your users to use them.

781
00:57:34,440 --> 00:57:38,190
We have covered a lot of ground over here, Saurabh.

782
00:57:38,190 --> 00:57:41,110
Before we go, I had two final questions.

783
00:57:41,110 --> 00:57:44,950
The first one is, if a listener is working at a company that's just starting to deploy LLM-based.

784
00:57:44,960 --> 00:57:51,470
features. what are the top three security considerations they should champion within their organization?

785
00:57:51,480 --> 00:58:00,390
The first consideration should be making sure that data that is being organized either through the user input.

786
00:58:00,400 --> 00:58:07,230
or being pulled from data sources does not have any kind of sensitive information. that is being sent out to the LLM.

787
00:58:07,240 --> 00:58:15,710
So, putting in basic content filters that detect sensitive information, that either block.

788
00:58:15,710 --> 00:58:19,950
or redact this information that goes out to the LLM is the basic most essential part of guardrail.

789
00:58:19,950 --> 00:58:21,470
that can be put in place.

790
00:58:21,480 --> 00:58:26,790
Then, the same kind of guardrails on data.

791
00:58:26,790 --> 00:58:32,511
that is coming out of the LLM back to the user can prevent accidental leakage of information to. the user.

792
00:58:32,511 --> 00:58:39,110
It might very well be that your application is about helping credit card users.

793
00:58:39,110 --> 00:58:42,270
and it is okay to reveal the last four digits of your credit card,

794
00:58:42,270 --> 00:58:44,710
but not the whole credit card in full text.

795
00:58:44,710 --> 00:58:51,910
So, putting in guardrails which can detect the sensitive piece of information, can block the input output.

796
00:58:51,910 --> 00:58:56,030
can do appropriate redaction are all the kinds of basic guardrails.

797
00:58:56,030 --> 00:58:57,830
that should at least be put in place to make sure.

798
00:58:57,830 --> 00:59:00,190
that you're not risking any kind of sensitive.

799
00:59:00,200 --> 00:59:02,870
information in an enterprise.

800
00:59:02,880 --> 00:59:08,110
Above and beyond keeping the application source the data,

801
00:59:08,110 --> 00:59:14,990
respecting the authorization levels that have been put in place is the second kind of essential guardrails.

802
00:59:14,990 --> 00:59:22,910
Data in enterprises is usually sitting in different kinds of applications which basically are protected by authentication authorization.

803
00:59:22,910 --> 00:59:26,990
but when the data is pulled in from these applications or central repository,

804
00:59:26,990 --> 00:59:30,030
these authentication authorization access controls are often overlooked.

805
00:59:30,040 --> 00:59:36,350
And so, when answering the question, it is very important to know understand what is the authentication authorization.

806
00:59:36,360 --> 00:59:41,350
level of the user, what data is being pulled.

807
00:59:41,350 --> 00:59:43,150
and is the data adhering to the authorization level.

808
00:59:43,150 --> 00:59:47,550
that was granted to the user in the first place in the source application before the answer can.

809
00:59:47,560 --> 00:59:49,511
be given back. to the user.

810
00:59:49,520 --> 00:59:55,470
That minimizes the risk of accidental excessive privileges.

811
00:59:55,470 --> 01:00:03,190
that could be exploited in any application to reveal unauthorized information back to the user.

812
01:00:03,190 --> 01:00:05,470
So, that would be another level of guardrail.

813
01:00:05,470 --> 01:00:09,510
that should be championed within an enterprise when you're writing an AI application.

814
01:00:09,510 --> 01:00:14,110
And then, the third is I'm going to go back to prompt engineering, and there are really two levels of prompt engineering.

815
01:00:14,110 --> 01:00:20,030
There is system prompt engineering where you can craft a very good system prompt in order to mitigate.

816
01:00:20,030 --> 01:00:22,110
some basic risks, and then there is context engineering.

817
01:00:22,110 --> 01:00:24,150
which is about how to organize the context.

818
01:00:24,150 --> 01:00:28,430
that is being given to the LLM along with the user input.

819
01:00:28,430 --> 01:00:32,750
how to represent that information to the LLM, how to minimize the risk in the context.

820
01:00:32,750 --> 01:00:35,670
and so on and so forth is kind of a a guardrail.

821
01:00:35,670 --> 01:00:41,230
that can be combined with all the above-mentioned guardrails in order to secure your AI application.

822
01:00:41,240 --> 01:00:44,910
Okay, so if I have to summarize and make sure I've got it right in my head.

823
01:00:44,910 --> 01:00:49,470
the top three security considerations would be First is to ensure.

824
01:00:49,470 --> 01:00:51,830
that you vet the data available to the LLM.

825
01:00:51,830 --> 01:00:58,630
The second would be to ensure that the guardrails we have discussed and talked about are implemented.

826
01:00:58,630 --> 01:01:00,990
And the third one is to ensure the access controls for the data.

827
01:01:01,000 --> 01:01:07,390
when you bring it in into the LLM's context, make sure the access control is retained.

828
01:01:07,400 --> 01:01:10,671
And honored. And honored, yes.

829
01:01:10,671 --> 01:01:15,470
So, if user A was not authorized to certain documents.

830
01:01:15,470 --> 01:01:21,510
let's make sure that the AI application is not pulling context contextual data to the user's question from.

831
01:01:21,510 --> 01:01:23,230
documents that the user was not authorized in the first place.

832
01:01:23,240 --> 01:01:24,990
Perfect.

833
01:01:24,990 --> 01:01:29,670
Any final thoughts or predictions about the future of AI security and prompt injection defense?

834
01:01:29,680 --> 01:01:35,750
Yeah, I mean, AI is a very fast-evolving landscape.

835
01:01:35,750 --> 01:01:39,750
We have seen vast amount of changes coming to light very, very quickly.

836
01:01:39,760 --> 01:01:44,230
We started off with basic AI applications, rag applications,

837
01:01:44,230 --> 01:01:52,831
where we are kind of leveraging enterprise data to answer user questions on enterprise use cases. and so on and so forth.

838
01:01:52,831 --> 01:01:57,470
Then we saw agentic architectures evolve really quickly.

839
01:01:57,470 --> 01:02:02,030
where you can build abilities for piece of code to take autonomous actions.

840
01:02:02,030 --> 01:02:09,390
connect with external systems in real time, not just be able to pull information, but act on information.

841
01:02:09,390 --> 01:02:12,030
It can take actions like creating tickets.

842
01:02:12,030 --> 01:02:13,550
or closing tickets or sending an email and so on and so forth.

843
01:02:13,560 --> 01:02:19,670
So, we saw evolution of AI where they are becoming way more actionable.

844
01:02:19,670 --> 01:02:25,310
and are able to materialize an end-to-end use case very, very effectively.

845
01:02:25,310 --> 01:02:31,510
And then, as these creative architectures are coming to light, new protocols are coming to light.

846
01:02:31,510 --> 01:02:35,310
you know, MCP became very, very popular in the last 6 to 9 months, I would say.

847
01:02:35,310 --> 01:02:37,990
although Anthropic was putting it forward for a few years.

848
01:02:38,000 --> 01:02:44,870
And approaches like MCP really help evolve agentic architectures where they can evolve very rapidly,

849
01:02:44,870 --> 01:02:48,070
you know, the tool implementers can independently implement tools on MCP servers.

850
01:02:48,070 --> 01:02:50,430
and agents can focus on the business logic.

851
01:02:50,440 --> 01:02:54,270
And then, once the agents and MCP servers kind of came to light,

852
01:02:54,270 --> 01:02:58,390
the further evolution of things like agent-to-agent architecture.

853
01:02:58,390 --> 01:03:04,270
or ability for agents to collaborate in a multi-agentic architectures, all of these architectures are evolving.

854
01:03:04,270 --> 01:03:08,870
and with each evolution, there are new kinds of attacks that are coming to light.

855
01:03:08,870 --> 01:03:14,751
You know, as with agentic architecture, it was all about keeping agent within the boundaries of what it. is supposed to do.

856
01:03:14,760 --> 01:03:19,550
With MCP, the attack surface shifted more towards the MCP server.

857
01:03:19,550 --> 01:03:24,830
and its ability and its independent evolution to the agent and so on and so forth.

858
01:03:24,830 --> 01:03:29,110
So, as the architectures evolving and coming to light.

859
01:03:29,110 --> 01:03:31,110
there are definitely new attack surfaces that are coming to light.

860
01:03:31,110 --> 01:03:34,030
that we have to take into account when designing these applications.

861
01:03:34,030 --> 01:03:37,150
and make sure that we are incorporating the right guardrails.

862
01:03:37,150 --> 01:03:42,430
and putting the right protection measures in order to prevent these risks from taking effect on enterprises.

